{0 WebSockets}

Use {!Workero.Websocket.upgrade} to accept a WebSocket connection and echo
messages. Return a normal response for requests without [Upgrade: websocket].

{@ocaml[
let handler req _env ctx =
  let open Workero in
  match Request.header req "upgrade" with
  | Some value when String.lowercase_ascii value = "websocket" ->
      Websocket.upgrade ctx (fun ws ->
          let rec loop () =
            match Websocket.receive ws with
            | Message (Text s) ->
                Websocket.send ws (Text ("echo: " ^ s));
                loop ()
            | Message (Binary b) ->
                Websocket.send ws (Binary b);
                loop ()
            | Close _ -> Websocket.close ws
            | Failed _ -> loop ()
          in
          loop ())
  | _ -> Response.text ~status:`Upgrade_Required "WebSocket upgrade required\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

Run [npx wrangler dev]. In a browser console on another local page, run:

{@javascript[
const ws = new WebSocket("ws://localhost:8787/");
ws.onmessage = (event) => console.log(event.data);
ws.onopen = () => ws.send("hello");
]}

The console prints [echo: hello]. A normal [curl -i http://localhost:8787/]
request gets status 426. See the
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/examples/websockets/}Cloudflare WebSocket example}
for the platform handshake and lifecycle.

{!Workero.Websocket.upgrade} starts your function in its own fiber through
{!Workero.Context.wait_until}. Synchronous work before the first suspension
can delay the [101] response.

- When your function returns, the socket closes with code 1000, if it is still
  open.
- If your function raises, the socket closes with code 1011. The error goes to
  [console.error].

{1 Events}

{!Workero.Websocket.receive} gives the next {!Workero.Websocket.event}. It
waits while no event is ready.

- [Message (Text s)] and [Message (Binary b)]: a message. Binary data is a
  [string] of bytes.
- [Close { code; reason; was_clean }]: the peer closed the connection. A second
  {!Workero.Websocket.receive} gives the same [Close].
- [Failed message]: the [error] event. A [Close] follows it.

Events come in the order they arrived. The queue starts when
{!Workero.Websocket.accept} runs, so no message is lost between the accept and
your first {!Workero.Websocket.receive}.

Only one fiber may wait in {!Workero.Websocket.receive}. A second concurrent
call raises [Invalid_argument].

The loop can call {!Workero.fetch} or {!Workero.Promise.await} between messages.
See {{!page-async}Async and background work}.

{1 Send and close}

- {!Workero.Websocket.send} sends [Text s] or [Binary b]. It raises
  {!Workero.Js_error} when the socket is closed.
- {!Workero.Websocket.close} closes with an optional [code] and [reason].

{1 Without upgrade}

{!Workero.Websocket.upgrade} is {!Workero.Websocket.val-pair},
{!Workero.Websocket.accept} and {!Workero.Websocket.response} in one call. Use
the parts to keep the [client] end, or to start the loop yourself.

{1 Durable Objects}

For connections that survive a Durable Object instance being evicted, use
{!Workero.Durable_object.State.accept_websocket} and the object's WebSocket
handlers. See {{!page-durable_objects}Durable Objects} for hibernation and
per-connection attachments.
